  "notes": "The neotraditional pole of 90s radio — Telecaster-and-steel honky-tonk arranged for arenas, windowed from the 1990 album onward as the entry specifies. Keys follow the research directly: \"overwhelmingly G/A/D/E with E♭ and F♯ appearing where the vocal needs it\" and \"readings cluster G major more than any other key,\" so G carries the top weight. The drum grammar is the stated two-beat — \"kick 1 and 3, snare 2&4 with the hat opening on 4\" — which is `anchors` 1/3 with `openHat.pos` on beat 4 at a high probability, plus cross-stick verses on the ballads. Harmony stays inside the stated \"I–IV–V and I–V–vi–IV with heavy IV-chord dwelling\"; the honky-tonk \"V7\" and \"I–I7–IV blues turn\" are expressed as a seventh weighting and a major_blues scale rather than as quality-bearing numerals, which the numeral parser cannot read. The long instrumental is a real structural fact — the entry says Jackson \"prioritised extended instrumental passages suitable for dance settings\" — so the bridge slot is authored 8–16 bars. ⚠ Boundary flag carried from the research: the fast catalogue (\"Chattahoochee,\" \"Don't Rock the Jukebox,\" \"Mercury Blues\") is train-beat/shuffle territory and belongs to the sibling archetype genres; the artist and the stated 70–150 band stay here. ⚠ The catalogue tempo readings are [LOW-CONF] per the section's own convention. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the melody register and density, the countermelody ratio and the humanize values — \"vocal sits low in the register\" and \"steel and fiddle answering every vocal phrase\" are directions, and 55–74, 3–6 and 0.62 are this author's reading of them.",
